<article class="env-remark" id="0G6W">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0G6W">Remark <span data-tag="0G6W">20.31.2</span>.</a> Let $(X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ be a ringed space. Let $K, M$ be objects of $D(\mathcal{O}_ X)$. Set $A = \Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$. Given $\xi \in H^ i(X, K)$ we get an associated map </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xi = ``\xi \cup -'' : R\Gamma (X, M)[-i] \to R\Gamma (X, K \otimes _{\mathcal{O}_ X}^\mathbf {L} M)  \]
</div>
  <p> by representing $\xi $ as a map $\xi : A[-i] \to R\Gamma (X, K)$ as in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/0FP2" data-tag="0FP2">20.31.1</a> and then using the composition </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  R\Gamma (X, M)[-i] = A[-i] \otimes _ A^\mathbf {L} R\Gamma (X, M) \xrightarrow {\xi \otimes 1} R\Gamma (X, K) \otimes _ A^\mathbf {L} R\Gamma (X, M) \to R\Gamma (X, K \otimes _{\mathcal{O}_ X}^\mathbf {L} M)  \]
</div>
  <p> where the second arrow is the global cup product $\mu $ above. On cohomology this recovers the cup product by $\xi $ as is clear from Lemma <a href="/tag/0FP2" data-tag="0FP2">20.31.1</a> and its proof. </p>
</article>